{"label": "in~4000", "t_monotonic": 22781.682438171, "t_monotonic_end": 22782.726374076, "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "status": "ok", "http_status": 200, "first_token_ms": 997.605338, "total_ms": 1043.826463, "tokens_in": 2744, "tokens_out": 164, "reasoning_tokens": 5, "reasoning_text": "Need short paragraph.", "tokens_estimated": false, "text": "A B‑tree is a balanced, sorted tree data structure in which each node stores multiple keys and pointers to child nodes, allowing the tree to remain shallow even with millions of records. Because the keys are kept in order, range queries (e.g., “find all values between X and Y”) can be answered by following a single path to the start key and then scanning sequentially through the leaf nodes, which are linked for fast in‑order traversal. Hash indexes, by contrast, map keys to a single bucket and lose any inherent ordering, so they can’t efficiently support range scans without a costly full‑table scan. Thus databases favor B‑trees for index structures that need to handle both point lookups and range queries.", "chunks": ["A", " B", "‑", "tree", " is", " a", " balanced", ",", " sorted", " tree", " data", " structure", " in", " which", " each", " node", " stores", " multiple", " keys", " and", " pointers", " to", " child", " nodes", ",", " allowing", " the", " tree", " to", " remain", " shallow", " even", " with", " millions", " of", " records", ".", " Because", " the", " keys", " are", " kept", " in", " order", ",", " range", " queries", " (", "e", ".g", ".,", " “", "find", " all", " values", " between", " ", "X", " ", "and", " ", "Y", "”)", " can", " be", " answered", " by", " following", " a", " single", " path", " to", " the", " start", " key", " and", " then", " scanning", " sequential", "ly", " through", " the", " leaf", " nodes", ",", " which", " are", " linked", " for", " fast", " in", "‑", "order", " traversal", ".", " Hash", " indexes", ",", " by", " contrast", ",", " map", " keys", " to", " a", " single", " bucket",