{"label": "qwen~1000#1", "t_monotonic": 22784.67106522, "t_monotonic_end": 22786.736857013, "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "status": "ok", "http_status": 200, "first_token_ms": 2015.228402, "total_ms": 2065.701763, "tokens_in": 726, "tokens_out": 245, "reasoning_tokens": 57, "reasoning_text": "The user is asking a straightforward technical question about B-trees and why databases prefer them over hash indexes for range queries. The repeated text before the question appears to be filler/padding and is irrelevant to the actual question. I'll provide a clear, concise one-paragraph answer.\n", "tokens_estimated": false, "text": "A B-tree is a self-balancing, multi-way search tree that maintains its data in sorted order, storing multiple keys per node and ensuring all leaf nodes sit at the same depth, which keeps lookup, insertion, and deletion to O(log n) time. Databases use B-trees rather than hash indexes for range queries (e.g., \"find all temperatures between 15 °C and 100 °C\") because a hash index can only tell you where a *specific* key lives—it gives you no information about the relative ordering of other keys—so once you locate one matching row you must scan the rest of the table. A B-tree, by contrast, keeps keys in sorted sequence along its leaves, so after descending to the lower bound of a range you can simply walk forward through adjacent leaf nodes until you hit the upper bound, touching only the relevant rows in a single, sequential pass.", "chunks": ["A", " B", "-tree", " is", " a", " self", "-bal", "ancing", ",", " multi", "-way", " search", " tree", " that", " maintains", " its", " data", " in", " sorted", " order", ",", " storing", " multiple", " keys", " per", " node", " and", " ensuring", " all", " leaf", " nodes", " sit", " at", " the", " same", " depth", ",", " which", " keeps", " lookup", ",", " insertion", ",", " and", " deletion", " to", " O", "(log", " n", ")", " time", ".", " D", "atabases", " use", " B", "-t", "rees", " rather", " than", " hash", " indexes", " for", " range", " queries", " (", "e", ".g", ".,", " \"", "find", " all", " temperatures", " between", " ", "1", "5", " °", "C", " and", " ", "1", "0", "0", " °", "C", "\")", " because", " a", " hash", " index", " can", " only", " tell", " you", " where", " a", " *", "specific", "*", " key", " lives", "—it", " gives", " you", " no", " information", " about", " the", " relative", " ordering", " of", " other", " keys", "—", "so", " once", " you", " locate", " one", " matching", " row", " you", " must", " scan", " the", " rest", " of", " the", " table", ".", " A", " B", "-tree", ",", " by", " contrast", ",", " keeps", " keys", " in", " sorted", " sequence", " along", " its", " leaves", ",", " so", " after", " descending", " to", " the", " lower", " bound", " of", " a", " range", " you", " can", " simply", " walk", " forward", " through", " adjacent", " leaf", " nodes", " until", " you", " hit", " the", " upper", " bound", ",", " touching", " only", " the", " relevant", " rows", " in", " a", " single", ",", " sequential", " pass", "."], "retry_after": null, "ratelimit_headers": {"rpd_limit": "1000", "tpm_limit": "8000", "rpd_remaining": "999", "tpm_remaining": "7022", "rpd_reset": "1m26.4s", "tpm_reset": "7.335s"}, "retries": 0, "max_tokens": 320, "finish_reason": "stop", "usage": {"queue_time": 0.641817933, "prompt_tokens": 726, "prompt_time": 0.058805893, "completion_tokens": 245, "completion_time": 0.535818714, "total_tokens": 971, "total_time": 0.594624607, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 57}}, "error_body": null, "headers": {"date": "Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:07:10 GMT", "content-type": "text/event-stream", "transfer-encoding": "chunked", "connection": "keep-alive", "cache-control": "no-cache", "server": "cloudflare", "vary": "Origin", "x-groq-region": "bom", "x-ratelimit-limit-requests": "1000", "x-ratelimit-limit-tokens": "8000", "x-ratelimit-remaining-requests": "999", "x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens": "7022", "x-ratelimit-reset-requ