{"label": "qwen~1000#2", "t_monotonic": 22786.737516289, "t_monotonic_end": 22787.440509034, "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "status": "ok", "http_status": 200, "first_token_ms": 409.629733, "total_ms": 702.903685, "tokens_in": 726, "tokens_out": 222, "reasoning_tokens": 67, "reasoning_text": "The user is asking me to explain what a B-tree is and why databases use it instead of a hash index for range queries, in one short paragraph. The repeated text before the question appears to be filler or a test of whether I'll be distracted by it. I'll ignore the repeated text and answer the actual question directly.\n", "tokens_estimated": false, "text": "A B-tree is a self-balancing tree data structure that maintains sorted data and allows searches, insertions, and deletions in logarithmic time; each node can hold multiple keys and multiple child pointers, making it well-suited for reading and writing large blocks of data from disk. Databases prefer B-trees over hash indexes for range queries (e.g., \"find all rows where price BETWEEN 10 AND 50\") because a B-tree's keys are stored in sorted order along the tree, so once the starting key is located, the database can simply traverse the leaf nodes sequentially to collect all matching rows—something a hash index cannot do, since hashing destroys ordering and only supports exact-match lookups by jumping directly to a single bucket.", "chunks": ["A", " B", "-tree", " is", " a", " self", "-bal", "ancing", " tree", " data", " structure", " that", " maintains", " sorted", " data", " and", " allows", " searches", ",", " insert", "ions", ",", " and", " delet", "ions", " in", " logarith", "mic", " time", ";", " each", " node", " can", " hold", " multiple", " keys", " and", " multiple", " child", " pointers", ",", " making", " it", " well", "-su", "ited", " for", " reading", " and", " writing", " large", " blocks", " of", " data", " from", " disk", ".", " D", "atabases", " prefer", " B", "-t", "rees", " over", " hash", " indexes", " for",