{"label": "cache-1500#1", "t_monotonic": 22787.441276922, "t_monotonic_end": 22788.439059492, "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "status": "ok", "http_status": 200, "first_token_ms": 957.304553, "total_ms": 997.653471, "tokens_in": 1089, "tokens_out": 149, "reasoning_tokens": 5, "reasoning_text": "Need short paragraph.", "tokens_estimated": false, "text": "A B‑tree is a balanced multiway search tree in which each node can hold multiple keys and pointers to child nodes, keeping all leaf nodes at the same depth. This structure allows quick navigation to any key by following a small number of levels, while keeping the tree height low even for millions of records. Databases use B‑trees instead of hash indexes for range queries because B‑trees store keys in sorted order, enabling efficient sequential access and range scans (e.g., “find all records where age is between 30 and 40”). Hash indexes, by contrast, map keys to a single bucket and lose ordering, making range queries require a full table scan.", "chunks": ["A", " B", "‑", "tree", " is", " a", " balanced", " multi", "way", " search", " tree", " in", " which", " each", " node", " can", " hold", " multiple", " keys", " and", " pointers", " to", " child", " nodes", ",", " keeping", " all", " leaf", " nodes", " at", " the", " same", " depth", ".", " This", " structure", " allows", " quick", " navigation", " to", " any", " key", " by", " following", " a", " small", " number", " of", " levels", ",", " while", " keeping", " the", " tree", " height", " low", " even", " for", " millions", " of", " records", ".", " Dat", "abases", " use", " B", "‑", "trees", " instead", " of", " hash", " indexes", " for", " range", " queries", " because", " B", "‑", "trees", " store", " keys", " in", " sorted", " order", ",", " enabling", " efficient", " sequential", " access", " and", " range", " scans", " (", "e", ".g", ".,", " “", "find", " all", " records", " where", " age", " is", " between", " ", "30", " and", " ", "40", "”).", " Hash", " 