{"label": "cache-1500#2-retry", "t_monotonic": 22837.646702709, "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "status": "ok", "http_status": 200, "first_token_ms": 485.515021, "total_ms": 710.310038, "tokens_in": 1089, "tokens_out": 163, "reasoning_tokens": 5, "reasoning_text": "Need concise paragraph.", "tokens_estimated": false, "text": "A B\u2011tree is a balanced multiway search tree in which each node can hold many keys and pointers to child nodes, keeping the tree shallow and all leaf nodes at the same depth. Because keys are stored in sorted order within the nodes, a B\u2011tree allows efficient range scans\u2014retrieving all records between two key values\u2014by following a path to the first key and then sequentially traversing sibling leaf nodes. Hash indexes, in contrast, distribute keys uniformly based on a hash function but lose ordering information, making range queries infeasible; they are only good for exact\u2011match lookups. Databases therefore employ B\u2011trees for primary and secondary indexes to support both point queries and fast range scans while keeping disk I/O minimal.", "chunks": ["A", " B", "\u2011", "tree", " is", " a", " balanced", " multi", "way", " search", " tree", " in", " which", " each", " node", " can", " hold", " many", " keys", " and", " pointers", " to", " child", " nodes", ",", " keeping", " the", " tree", " shallow", " and", " all", " leaf", " nodes", " at", " the", " same", " depth", ".", " Because", " keys", " are", " stored", " in", " sorted", " order", " within", " the", " nodes", ",", " a", " B", "\u2011", "tree", " allows", " efficient", " range", " scans", "\u2014", "retr", "ieving", " all", " records", " between", " two", " key", " values", "\u2014", "by", " following", " a", " path", " to", " the", " first", " key", " and", " then", " sequential", "ly", " travers", "ing", " sibling", " leaf", " nodes", ".", " Hash", " indexes", ",", " in", " contrast", ",", " distribute", " keys", " uniformly", " based", " on", " a", " hash", 